// Pools for the raw memory table made resident a few at a time, ahead of the table (DM_NVN_RES_TRICKLE_MB=<MB>, off by default since the A/B under DM_NVN_PREREAD: each submission walked every pool under g_mu, with more lock waits; 16 is the fork's). Ported from kid-with-sword-remake (cheemu21, MIT)
// L7.8, c8c37258. Once a game has used raw global memory (a pool table built), every pool something gave an address in goes into the residency set when the table is next built: in the
// fork's iPhone 18 Pro log 2,453 pools, 1,176 MB, in the commit of the frame that first drew a new area, which stopped 784 ms. The game addresses them while it loads, frames before that
// draw, so each submission here puts up to this many MB of them into the set (pool_res's way: marked under g_mu, committed after it); the table then finds them resident and adds what is
// left. The same pools the table would add (addr_meets), so memory ends where it would, only sooner, and no lookup changes. Pools over 64 MB and large pools' chunks are left to the table,
// which gives a large pool one whole buffer (big_full). Runs after the unwire sweep, so a pool it takes out is not put back in the same submission
