static void treg_drop_pool_views(const void *pool);
static void cpu_tex_drop_pool(const void *pool);
static void gws_drop_pool(const pool_t *p);
static void rmu_lock(void); static void rmu_unlock(void);
// On memory pool Finalize, its texture IDs are marked gone, then the pool's textures and the serial entries that name them go (pool_finalize_locked says why), its packaged textures
// leave g_pkg_share (a later pool at this address holds other bytes) and its image descriptions go, all in one hold of g_rmu (taken before g_smu). A reader holding g_rmu (a pool walk,
// pool_gpu; a handle binding) resolves the pool's IDs before it, while their entries stand, or after it, when they are gone and read as empty slots (tex_made). The entries used to end
// in a hold of g_smu alone, and the IDs to be marked in a later one: a walk in between found an ID not gone and the pool's magic still set, and tex_mtl_, finding no entry, made the
// texture again, put under its live serial (which had left the pool index, so nothing ended it) and in the sharing table under the dead pool's key. It stayed until the object's own
// Finalize: Ocarina of Time's 288 MB shadow target through Hyrule Field, the hold the Finalize ends. Marked gone in a hold of its own first, a reader in between would take tex_made, find
// the entries still there and cache a view of one in the view cache (vc_put), kept until the object's own Finalize too. A texture object resolved outside g_rmu finds the magic cleared
// before this (nvnMemoryPoolFinalize), and one that read it just before puts nothing: the Finalize is counted in this hold (pool_fin_note), and each of that resolution's puts finds
// its pool finalized since the reading it took before the magic (pool_fin_since), whatever has become of the serial meanwhile
void mtl_alias_drop_pool(const void *pool) { @autoreleasepool {
