// A sparse texture's CPU-write check walks its page table (nvn_sparse_since). A submission sees the CPU writes and the mappings made before it (as NVN's: a map is a CPU call,
// not a command), so the replay checks each one once a submission (sub: the submission, g_sub_serial, of its last check). Games remap streamed pages all the time, so the
// mapping generation alone rarely let the check be skipped: Mario Kart World's terrain targets walked theirs at nearly every lookup. Since the change log (pkg_sparse_clean,
// DM_NVN_SPARSE_LOG) the walk is made only when a mapping request overlaps the texture; otherwise the runs of physical memory the last walk found are looked at
// ---------- Mip drop (DM_NVN_MIP_DROP) ----------
// A memory and bandwidth option for the phone, off by default: a large block-compressed texture the host uploads from its storage is made without its top K levels (1 or 2), at a
// quarter or a sixteenth of the bytes, and those levels are neither deswizzled nor copied. Someone running Ocarina of Time on a 2 GB Apple TV did the same (two levels of eligible
// compressed textures); NX 18's release-2 notes listed it as R1. A shader's sample with derivatives picks the same level of the game's texture it would have (the host texture's level
// l is the game's level l + K, and its size is the game's halved K times, so the computed level falls by K too), down to the first level kept: the image loses only the finest detail.
// Eligible (md_pick): an upload the replay asks for while sampling through a texture ID (tex_by_id, t_md_sample), of a 2D texture or 2D array in a BC or ASTC format, no flag but
