// both sides even K times over (so the host's level 0 is the game's level K exactly) and keeping a whole block where the game's size has whole blocks, with every level down to 4
// texels or fewer and no more than its size holds, in a pool that is not sparse, never drawn into (no render-target or image use carried over). Every other host use of the texture
// (a copy from or into it, a readback, a clear, an attachment, an image binding, a capture: every tex_mtl outside tex_by_id) names levels by the game's numbers: the texture is made
// whole first (md_whole, from its storage, which holds its content as for any re-upload) and stays whole (md_no). A texture ID's view of levels [b, b + n) takes the host's
// [b - K, b + n - K), at least its first level (tex_by_id). Not handled, and why the switch is off by default until a device A/B: a shader's integer read (texelFetch / read()),
// size query (textureSize / get_width) or level count of such a texture gets the host texture's, and an explicit level (textureLod) or a sampler's level clamp counts from the host's
// first level, K levels blurrier than asked; the translated shaders say nothing of which textures they read so (docs/reference/memory.md, "Top mip levels left out on the phone")
