// ---------- Live textures idle at low headroom (DM_NVN_IDLE_TEX_MB; ported from S2Arm 8adc5673, 987b31ef, Apache-2.0) ----------
// A live texture object's host texture stays until the game finalizes the object, and an open world keeps thousands of objects alive that it no longer draws with: S2Arm measured
// Ocarina of Time on iOS at 345 MB of host textures at frame 600 and 3.1 GB at frame 80,700. On the Switch such an object is a descriptor over guest memory and costs nothing more.
// So while the headroom to the system memory limit (g_rh_avail: os_proc_available_memory, none on the Mac, read at a commit at most every TIDLE_EVERY_NS) is under the threshold,
// the live entries whose content the guest storage still holds go, least recently used first, until about twice the threshold is free again. One qualifies when:
//  - every texture ID registered with its serial was bound (treg_t used, g_tu_now's clock: tex_fast, tex_fast_rid, tex_by_id, a kept handle table) and none for g_tidle_ds (3 s),
//    and none is named by a pool table (tt1, set when a pool walk resolves the ID; a stand-in) — a table the GPU may read any frame without a binding resolving it;
//  - its storage is what the host takes content from (tex_storage_ok: a pool the CPU can read, not COMPRESSIBLE, LINEAR, sparse, multisampled; not display or video-decode) and the
//    GPU wrote none of it since the CPU last did (gws_owned: render targets, clears, copies, shader images over it), and its texture has no render-target or shader-write usage;
//    or it has a packaged layout, which the host also takes from its storage at every use (DM_NVN_IDLE_TEX_PKG, tidle_src_why), with no GPU store in its package; and mip drop
//    neither shrank it nor could (tidle_src_why, tidle_tex_w);
//  - nothing else names it: one serial entry alone (no sRGB twin's view, no finalized serial), no pin (queued descriptors, saved bindings), not a resolution family member, not a
//    view, a buffer texture or a sparse one.
// It goes as a Finalize's entry does: live_del_locked (its object behind this submission's fence through the deferred-release queue, borrow-safe for what encoding holds; a note), its
// IDs' views and its view-cache set (deferred unrefs), and its sharing-table entry when nothing else holds that (alias_del, as shared_idle_locked mode 1). The next use makes it
// again from guest memory (tex_mtl_). DM_NVN_IDLE_TEX_MB=<MB> sets the threshold (1536 by default), 0 turns it off; with DM_NVN_RT_DEAD=0 the ID clock stands still and none goes
enum { TIDLE_DS = 30 };
