static uint64_t g_tidle_n, g_tidle_bytes, g_tidle_runs, g_tidle_lines;   // released in all, their bytes, the sweeps that released any, the lines logged
// The footprint cap (DM_NVN_IDLE_TEX_FOOT_MB): Ocarina of Time on the iPhone 18 Pro Max (12 GB; a464a49e6, NX18-X2.0 traces/oot-mst-1-findings.md) went from a 3.4 GB footprint to
// 5.5-5.8 GB at an area change, the graphics ledger 2.6 -> 4.3 GB and serial objects 2,673 -> 4,490 (the last area's textures, which the game keeps as live objects), the residency set
// 4.1 -> 5.1-5.7 GB, with 2.6 GB still to its memory limit, so the headroom threshold above never came near. The phone itself was short: the kernel paged and compressed on about
// half a core, the guest's files were read back in (17 GB of page-ins), and those page-ins were the slow residency commits' time. So the same sweep also runs while the footprint
// (task_vm_info phys_footprint) is over the cap, and releases the least recently used idle textures until it is about an eighth under the cap. What qualifies and how one goes is
// the same as above: only textures whose content the guest storage holds, made again from it at their next use, and resident again before the command buffer that uses them is
// committed (tex_mtl_'s res_add, then the guards). By default on iOS two fifths of the device's memory (4,915 MB on 12 GB, 3,277 MB on 8 GB); none on the Mac; =<MB> sets it, 0 off
