// Compaction: every start writes a file or more (each flush captures only what is new), Breath of the Wild 5-7 per start, Ocarina of Time 13-273 in a play session. A file with
// an index (see arch_key) costs a lookup nothing, one without (written before the index) about 0.06 ms for each lookup it misses, and a start opens every file (about 15 ms each,
// in parallel) and the disk keeps them. Run files are taken by size tier (arch_tier, from the pipeline count in the name), each PSO_ARCH_COMPACT times the one below; a full
// period's file is in none. When a tier has more than PSO_ARCH_COMPACT files, the bases the prewarm takes from it and every smaller tier are captured again from the original shader
// functions (the #342 workaround, a full compile each), into one file, or one per ARCH_PERIOD_MAX of them; once every period that may hold them is written, those files and their
// indexes are removed (open archives stay usable for the rest of the run). The prewarm only notes them (compact_note: their descriptors, as it found them); the captures run after
// the first frame, in the background (compact_step: one thread at utility QoS, COMPACT_BATCH at a time with a rest after each, held while the device is hot, in Low Power Mode or in
// the background), so a start never pays for them: Fortune's Weave's prepare step took 8.41 s with 447 bases captured again in it (37.2 s on 6 workers), Smash's 7.70 s with 866.
// With every run file in it, a start after a few files of play
// captured the whole archive again. Bases the prewarm did not take (pso_user.tsv never named them) go with them and are built again when drawn. The files without a vertex layout
// (p<seq>, pb<seq>_<program>) are left to the prebuild (pre_trim). A compaction that failed is not tried again on the same files (compact_failed.tsv: arch_compact_retry, in
// arch_compact_pick); one the process ended before it finished (a quit, iOS ending it in the background) left the old files as they were, and the next start notes them again
