// Captures that share a vertex-stage function (a render pipeline's vertex function, a mesh pipeline's object and mesh functions, a compute pipeline's kernel) never compile at the
// same time (upstream NXNative 75758b940). A capturing compile looks its program up in its period's archive (MultiLevelAirntBinaryCache::getElementAtDestination →
// -[_MTLBinaryArchive getBinaryDataForKey:reflectionType:]) and messages the entry it gets back without holding it, while another compile of the same program captures it and
// frees that entry: SIGSEGV in objc_msgSend at getElementAtDestination+60, the other workers in getBinaryDataForKey, and no archive write under way (upstream: Ocarina of Time's
// prebuild 3 runs of 3 on an M2 Max, GitHub issue #815, 1 of 6 on an M4 Pro; Breath of the Wild 1.9.0's once on an M4 Pro, macOS 27.2.1). The cause is in Metal; the prebuild's
// pairs came in list order, and consecutive ones often share a vertex shader (Ocarina of Time's lists: 18,308 of 34,649 pairs, up to 996 in a row). A function is its library
// and entry name, hashed: a collision only makes two compiles wait for each other. The library by its bytes (lib_sum), as Metal keys a program by its content: two shader keys
// whose libraries are the same bytes (shared sources compile alike, from standard input: shaders.md, A library that depends on its source's file name; upstream found 4 keys
// with one library in Cuphead) are one program to Metal, and by name (lib_tag, the file) they were two claims that let both capture it at once. Upstream's lib_tag is that
// digest; NX 18's names the file, for the archive's index (arch_key). A library with no file by its name, else the object. The claims hold across flush
// periods, the draws' (g_ep) and the prebuild's (g_ep_pre) alike: the crash names the period's archive, but nothing shows Metal's binary cache is not shared, so the host takes
// no chance on it. The fragment function is not claimed: every crash compiled a vertex program (compileProgram<AGX::VertexProgramKey>), and Octopath Traveler II, whose 1,910
// pairs each have their own vertex shader and share a few pixel shaders, prebuilt on 14 workers in some 45 upstream runs without one; claiming it held that prebuild to one
// compile at a time (17 s → 118 s). A capture that cannot claim its functions never compiles beside the other: the prebuild puts its pair back and builds it once they are free
// (pre_later_take), and every other caller (the draws, the prewarm and prepare step, the background builds) waits up to g_fn_wait_ms and then builds the pipeline with the
// plain compiler, which captures nothing and reads no period's archive (the archive misses it this run; it is captured at a later start). So a compile that does not return
// (#294) holds back a draw's capture of its own functions by that much, not for good, and none of them races it (t_pso_stall). A compaction's recapture and a harvest have their
// pipeline already (from the old file; built by the plain compiler for its first use), so they never build it again: one that cannot claim its functions is not captured, and
