// ---- The archive bundled with the app, opened early ----
// Metal's open of the bundled archive (arch_open_read: the read-ahead of upstream PR #2657, then loadFromURL, which hashes every Metal library in the file on the calling thread)
// sat on the path to the first frame: pso4_init runs at the game's first submission (warm_start), and on an iPhone 18 Pro Max (build 431d5d18, with the read-ahead) the open took
// 2.35 s for Ocarina of Time's 4.6 GB archive, 1.2 s for Super Mario RPG's 2.5 GB, 1.1 s for Donkey Kong Bananza's 1.8 GB, 0.9 s for Super Mario Bros. Wonder's 2.2 GB and
// 0.5-0.6 s for Mario Kart World's, Smash Ultimate's, Odyssey's and Breath of the Wild's (0.8-1.5 GB). So the game's start hands that open to a background queue: main.c sets
// dm_mem_ready_hook, which hle_svc.c calls once the guest's address space is reserved (mem_init_once, at the game's first memory call, the start of nnosInitialize; the launcher's
// settings are in the environment by then), and pso_bundle_open takes the archive at the first submission, waiting only for what is left of the open: it runs beside the game's
// own start (its modules' init, nnMain, its file system and graphics init, the shader map and libraries) instead of after it. After the guest's block, not before it, as before:
// Metal maps the whole file, and the two largest mappings keep their order in the address space. The same file, container check, crash guard (its record written before Metal
// reads the file, as before, only earlier, then put back until the first submission takes the archive: pso_guard_idle), read-ahead and open; the archive is the same object
// every lookup asks, so nothing a game sees changes. The device is the system
// default one, the object mtl_init takes later (MTLCreateSystemDefaultDevice gives the same; a different one at the join opens the file again on g_dev). Not for a GPU capture
// run (no archive), DM_PSO_BUNDLE=off, a GPU without a file, or a file under PSO_PREOPEN_MIN (tens of ms to open: the first submission opens it as before), nor when the game
// asks for no memory before its first submission. DM_PSO_PREOPEN=0 turns it off (the A/B; the 'opened in' line then says nothing of a wait).
// The open runs on a serial queue of its own at user-initiated QoS, below the game's threads (user-interactive, QoS 0x21 on the iPhone), so it takes what they leave of the CPU;
// the first submission waits for its block with dispatch_block_wait, which raises that queue, and the thread running the block, to the waiting thread's QoS for what is left of
// the open (Metal's hashing, CPU bound). A dispatch_group_wait (the first version) raises nothing, nor does a wait on a block of a global queue (libdispatch overrides only a
// serial queue), so what was left of the open ran below the threads still busy with the game's start, on the path to the first frame (a priority inversion)
enum { PSO_PREOPEN_MIN = 256 << 20 };
static uint64_t g_preopen_min = PSO_PREOPEN_MIN;   // t_pso_bundle lowers it for its small archive
static struct {
    _Atomic int state;   // 0 none yet; 1 under way or done (done says when); 2 taken by the first submission (pso_bundle_join), or no file for this GPU; 3 the first submission came
