    MLOG("pipeline archive %s: %d files the prebuild just wrote (%d with an index of %lu pipelines), %.1f MB, opened in %.1f ms before the first frame",
         g_arch_dir.UTF8String, nopen, nidx, nkeys, obytes / 1048576.0, (bp_now() - t0) / 1e6);
}
// The prebuild in the prepare step: the bases of every shader pair are made before the game's first frame, behind the progress screen ("Preparing pipelines"), once per chain
// version, depth mode and pair list (prebuilt.tsv), so a running game never compiles them meanwhile. Built in the background while playing, they took an iPhone 17 Pro Max's GPU
// from about 13 to 15-18 ms a frame in Breath of the Wild (the compiler's load on the shared power budget); a first start waits for them instead. It waits until every pair is
// handed to a worker and none is put back (pre_pair: a pair whose shader function another capture had waits in g_pre_later, and counts as not yet built), then for the
// compiles still under way (at most one per worker) for g_pre_tail_ms at most, and for the last period's write once every worker has returned (g_pre_flushing), so that
// arch_adopt_prebuilt opens the files the prebuild wrote before the first frame (upstream NXNative 4add5fbb4). A compile that does not return (a base Apple's compiler cannot
// build, GitHub issue #294) so holds the game's start g_pre_tail_ms at most, and its prebuild finishes in the background, its files opened at the next start. Nor do the pairs
// put back behind such a compile hold it: once every pair is handed out, a step in which no pair has finished for g_pre_prep_idle_ms leaves them to the background too, with no
// tail wait (a pair's base takes some 0.2 s on a Mac's prebuild workers; upstream waits for them with no limit). The tests lower both
enum { PRE_PREP_IDLE_MS = 10000, PRE_TAIL_MS = 10000 };
