// The depth rule (above) for a draw whose pass has a depth attachment; dsw holds the planes written by this draw. Run before the colour attachments' snapshots and tile reads, which need the pass
// this decides on
// One snapshot a barrier interval for the draws that only read the plane (DM_NVN_FB_DSNAP, on by default): Donkey Kong Bananza's effects chain over the main view has depth-reading draws
// between draws that write depth, and the rule above ended its pass 6 to 14 times a frame, each end storing and reloading every attachment (~100-130 MB a frame; NX18-X2.0
// bananza-alldata-analysis.md, fix 3). NVN makes rendering visible to texture reads only through a barrier, so, as for the writers above, one copy taken since the game's last barrier
// (in this submission) holds everything such a draw may expect to read: a read of a plane an earlier draw of the pass wrote takes the snapshot (its first one in the interval still
// ends the pass, to copy what is stored, as the rule did), and later reads in the interval reuse it without ending the pass, a depth write between them or not; a read with no
// earlier write in its pass reads a live snapshot too when there is one, so a later depth write does not end the pass for it (g_fb_dread). A depth or stencil clear, or a write
// other than drawing into a depth texture (a copy, a texture clear, an image binding), since the snapshot makes a fresh one (g_fb_dclr). Single handles in a uniform buffer (TEXU) are taken as slots
